::Q1::Which characteristic is most associated with a data warehouse? 
{
~Temporary data that is deleted after each transaction
=Data from multiple sources organized for analysis
~Only one application's current transaction records
~Only unstructured files with no defined purpose
}

::Q2::Why might an organization separate its analytical system from its operational systems? 
{
~Operational systems cannot store any data
=Analytical queries can be large and should not interfere with day-to-day transactions
~A warehouse is required for every database update
~Analytical queries never need historical data
}

::Q3::What does integrating data from multiple sources mean in a data warehouse context? 
{
=Combining data from different systems into a form suitable for analysis
~Deleting data whenever two systems contain similar values
~Converting every source into the same application
~Allowing only one source system to write data
}

::Q4::Why can a data warehouse contain data that is no longer current in the source system? 
{
=Historical information is retained so it can be analyzed over time
~Warehouses cannot receive new information
~Old data is always considered invalid
~Warehouses only store failed transactions
}

::Q5::Which statement best distinguishes a data warehouse from an operational database? 
{
~A warehouse cannot contain structured data, while an operational database can
=A warehouse is optimized for analysis, while an operational database supports day-to-day transactions
~A warehouse stores only passwords, while an operational database stores only sales
~A warehouse is always smaller than an operational database
}